#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-replay(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:7
msgid ""
"git-replay - EXPERIMENTAL: Replay commits on a new base, works with bare "
"repos too"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:13
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(EXPERIMENTAL!) 'git replay' ([--contained] --onto <newbase> | --advance "
"<branch>) <revision-range>...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:22
msgid ""
"Takes ranges of commits and replays them onto a new location. Leaves the "
"working tree and the index untouched, and updates no references.  The output "
"of this command is meant to be used as input to `git update-ref --stdin`, "
"which would update the relevant branches (see the OUTPUT section below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:24
msgid "THIS COMMAND IS EXPERIMENTAL. THE BEHAVIOR MAY CHANGE."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:28
#, no-wrap
msgid "--onto <newbase>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:31
msgid ""
"Starting point at which to create the new commits.  May be any valid commit, "
"and not just an existing branch name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:36
msgid ""
"When `--onto` is specified, the update-ref command(s) in the output will "
"update the branch(es) in the revision range to point at the new commits, "
"similar to the way how `git rebase --update-refs` updates multiple branches "
"in the affected range."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:37
#, no-wrap
msgid "--advance <branch>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:40
msgid "Starting point at which to create the new commits; must be a branch name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:44
msgid ""
"When `--advance` is specified, the update-ref command(s) in the output will "
"update the branch passed as an argument to `--advance` to point at the new "
"commits (in other words, this mimics a cherry-pick operation)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:45
#, no-wrap
msgid "<revision-range>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:51
msgid ""
"Range of commits to replay. More than one <revision-range> can be passed, "
"but in `--advance <branch>` mode, they should have a single tip, so that "
"it's clear where <branch> should point to. See \"Specifying Ranges\" in "
"linkgit:git-rev-parse[1] and the \"Commit Limiting\" options below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:55
#, no-wrap
msgid "OUTPUT"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:59
msgid ""
"When there are no conflicts, the output of this command is usable as input "
"to `git update-ref --stdin`.  It is of the form:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tupdate refs/heads/branch1 ${NEW_branch1_HASH} ${OLD_branch1_HASH}\n"
"\tupdate refs/heads/branch2 ${NEW_branch2_HASH} ${OLD_branch2_HASH}\n"
"\tupdate refs/heads/branch3 ${NEW_branch3_HASH} ${OLD_branch3_HASH}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:68
msgid ""
"where the number of refs updated depends on the arguments passed and the "
"shape of the history being replayed.  When using `--advance`, the number of "
"refs updated is always one, but for `--onto`, it can be one or more "
"(rebasing multiple branches simultaneously is supported)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXIT STATUS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:76
msgid ""
"For a successful, non-conflicted replay, the exit status is 0.  When the "
"replay has conflicts, the exit status is 1.  If the replay is not able to "
"complete (or start) due to some kind of error, the exit status is something "
"other than 0 or 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:78
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:81
msgid "To simply rebase `mybranch` onto `target`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git replay --onto target origin/main..mybranch\n"
"update refs/heads/mybranch ${NEW_mybranch_HASH} ${OLD_mybranch_HASH}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:88
msgid "To cherry-pick the commits from mybranch onto target:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:92
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git replay --advance target origin/main..mybranch\n"
"update refs/heads/target ${NEW_target_HASH} ${OLD_target_HASH}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:98
msgid ""
"Note that the first two examples replay the exact same commits and on top of "
"the exact same new base, they only differ in that the first provides "
"instructions to make mybranch point at the new commits and the second "
"provides instructions to make target point at them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:101
msgid ""
"What if you have a stack of branches, one depending upon another, and you'd "
"really like to rebase the whole set?"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git replay --contained --onto origin/main origin/main..tipbranch\n"
"update refs/heads/branch1 ${NEW_branch1_HASH} ${OLD_branch1_HASH}\n"
"update refs/heads/branch2 ${NEW_branch2_HASH} ${OLD_branch2_HASH}\n"
"update refs/heads/tipbranch ${NEW_tipbranch_HASH} ${OLD_tipbranch_HASH}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:112
msgid ""
"When calling `git replay`, one does not need to specify a range of commits "
"to replay using the syntax `A..B`; any range expression will do:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git replay --onto origin/main ^base branch1 branch2 branch3\n"
"update refs/heads/branch1 ${NEW_branch1_HASH} ${OLD_branch1_HASH}\n"
"update refs/heads/branch2 ${NEW_branch2_HASH} ${OLD_branch2_HASH}\n"
"update refs/heads/branch3 ${NEW_branch3_HASH} ${OLD_branch3_HASH}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/git-replay.txt:124
msgid ""
"This will simultaneously rebase `branch1`, `branch2`, and `branch3`, all "
"commits they have since `base`, playing them on top of `origin/main`. These "
"three branches may have commits on top of `base` that they have in common, "
"but that does not need to be the case."
msgstr ""

